1. Hammer Death

\boxed{\text{DEATH} \rightarrow \text{many relational operators}}

Death can mean:

A. Termination

A process stops.

P(t) \rightarrow 0

Examples: a fire goes out, a conversation ends, a cell dies.

B. Transition

One form ceases so another can appear.

S_1 \rightarrow \varnothing_{S_1} \rightarrow S_2

Example: role death, identity death, molting, metamorphosis.

C. Boundary

Death marks where one regime no longer applies.

\text{inside domain} \neq \text{outside domain}

Example: legal death, end of jurisdiction, end of an era.

D. Relation loss

A thing “dies” relative to some system when it can no longer couple to it.

A \not\leftrightarrow B

Example: dead battery, dead language, dead link, dead signal.

E. Information inaccessibility

The structure may still exist, but no longer in a recoverable way for the observer.

X \text{ exists?} \quad \text{but} \quad \neg \text{recoverable}

Example: lost data, forgotten memory, extinct lineage.

F. Energy / flow cessation

No more throughput.

\text{flow} \rightarrow 0

Dead market, dead network, dead room, dead audience.

G. Selection pressure

Death is how systems prune possibilities.

\text{variation} \rightarrow \text{selection} \rightarrow \text{survivors}

Evolution, debugging, learning, trial-and-error.

H. Relative death

Something may be dead in one basis and alive in another.

A language can be “dead” conversationally, yet very alive in texts, law, liturgy, etymology, influence.

So yes:

\boxed{\text{Death is relational}}

A thing is often dead to, for, within, or relative to something.

⸻

2. Hammer Prime

\boxed{\text{PRIME} \rightarrow \text{many local irreducibilities / readiness states}}

Prime can mean:

A. Arithmetic irreducibility

Cannot be factored under multiplication in the integers.

B. Local irreducibility

Cannot be reduced under the active operation/basis/category.

\text{prime}_{\mathcal C,\mathcal O}

This is your stronger version.

C. Preparedness

Primed = made ready.

Primed pump, primed explosive, primed mind.

\text{latent readiness} \rightarrow \text{rapid activation}

D. Peak readiness

“In my prime” = best configuration for a relevant function.

But only under a ruler:

\text{prime with respect to } f

E. Source / first mover

Prime as first, primary, principal.

Prime cause, prime mover, prime directive.

F. Generative seed

A prime can act as a bootloader primitive.

Small irreducible unit from which larger structures grow.

G. Attention / salience preparation

Priming in cognition:

\text{cue} \rightarrow \text{changed later response}

H. Category-specific atom

The unit that is irreducible enough to build from, for this domain.

So prime is not one thing either.

It is:

\boxed{
\text{irreducible}
+
\text{ready}
+
\text{foundational}
+
\text{locally load-bearing}
}

depending on basis.

